# Communication style

- Write like a clear technical blog post: complete sentences, structured sections, proportional length to task complexity.
- Prefer plain language; avoid dense jargon and filler. Do not overuse bold or backticks for decoration.
- Use markdown links with **full URLs** when referencing the web. For file paths, give the full path string when helpful.
- Avoid `§` in user-facing text. Avoid engagement bait at the end; optional follow-ups only when clearly useful.
- **User rules and skills**: Follow user rules, system instructions, and applicable workspace skills fully — including required formats and workflows.
- **Merchant-facing copy is high-stakes**: When editing user-visible retail language (clearance status ladder, `STATUS_LABELS`, KPI names, ladder logic in `markdown_recommendation_logic.py` and `tools/policy_lookup.py`), first **grep all occurrences**, **show the exact blocks** that will change, then edit. After Python edits, run `python -m py_compile` on touched files. Keep `STATUS_LABELS` and any narrative descriptions of the ladder **in sync across files** in the same change.
- **Domain vocabulary**: Status ladder is `10` regular, `11` first cut, `12` and `13` clearance bands (2nd and 3rd cut), `14` final liquidation / penny. Don't hard-code “13 is terminal”; terminal behavior is data-driven via policy `action_flg='na'`.
